@page "/grid"

<CatHead Title="Grid and layout">
    Primitives for placing markup you write: a wrapping grid of peers, a 24-column grid for
    panels, a main column beside an aside, and panels placed on cells that the reader can arrange.
    None of them cares what its children are — a card, a field, a figure or an element of your own.
    Each responds to the width it is given, not the window's, so the same markup is right on a page,
    in a drawer and inside a narrow card.
</CatHead>

<div class="callout callout--info">
    <i class="ri-layout-grid-line"></i>
    <div class="callout-body">
        <span class="callout-title">Which one</span>
        Children that are all the same kind and size — tiles, fields, thumbnails — go in
        <code>.sedna-grid</code>. Panels of different widths that you place yourself — a dashboard — go
        in <code>.sedna-grid-24</code>. Panels with a place, a width and a height each, that the reader
        moves and resizes, go in <code>.panel-grid</code>. One content column with a side panel is <code>.sedna-split</code>.
        A column of panels inside any of them is a <code>.sedna-stack</code>.
        Page width needs no wrapper: <code>.page</code> holds content to <code>--page-max</code>.
    </div>
</div>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Grid.Dashboard" Title="A dashboard" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        A <a href="/stat"><code>.stat-row</code></a> across the top, then two independent columns:
        a <code>.sedna-span-16</code> and a <code>.sedna-span-8</code>, each a <code>.sedna-stack</code>
        of panels. <strong>A panel growing in one column moves nothing in the other</strong>, which
        is what you want when the columns are unrelated — the main chart and table on one side, the
        queue on the other. A stack takes the grid's own gap.
    </p>
    <p>Resize the window to watch it collapse: the two columns stack below 48rem.</p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Interop.DashboardTiles" Title="Panels the reader arranges" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <strong>Live &mdash; drag a panel by its grip, or pull its bottom edge.</strong> A dashboard the
        reader puts in order is a <a href="/drag-drop"><code>.drag-grid</code></a> of
        <a href="/card"><code>.card--resizable</code></a> panels, with nothing added:
        <code>data-drag-axis="grid"</code> numbers the panels in reading order, and the app moves one
        in its own list on <code>sedna-drop</code>; each panel's <code>.card-resize</code> writes
        <code>--card-height</code>, and the app keeps it from <code>sedna-resize</code>. Store both per
        reader and render them back. <code>--drag-grid-min</code> on the grid is the narrowest a panel
        may be, which sets how many fit side by side. Where a panel needs a width of its own and a
        place beside and under others, it is a <code>.panel-grid</code>, below.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Grid.PanelGrid" Title="`.panel-grid`" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        Panels each placed on cells: <code>--panel-x</code> and <code>--panel-y</code> are the column and
        the row it starts on, from 0, and <code>--panel-w</code> and <code>--panel-h</code> how many it
        covers, written inline from the arrangement you store. <code>--panel-cols</code> (12) columns
        share the width and a row is <code>--panel-row</code> (3rem) tall. A panel stays where it is put,
        so a narrow one can sit beside a tall one under a wide one &mdash; and nothing stops two
        overlapping but the arrangement, which <code>SednaPanels.Arrange</code> settles.
    </p>
    <p>
        Narrower than 560px the panels stack, a whole row wide, in reading order.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Interop.PanelGridArrange" Title="A panel grid the reader arranges" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <strong>Live &mdash; drag a panel by its grip, pull its side, its bottom or its corner.</strong>
        The grid is a drag zone with <code>data-drag-axis="cells"</code>, arranged while it carries
        <code>data-arrange</code>: outside it the panels are content, and their grips and handles are not
        there. A panel in the way of the one carried goes down below it and every panel floats up, so
        nothing overlaps and nothing hangs over a gap; the grid marks the cell it would land on.
        <code>sedna-drop</code> and <code>sedna-resize</code> carry <code>Panels</code>, the whole
        arrangement as <code>SednaPanel</code>s in reading order: store it and render it back. Nothing is
        moved for you &mdash; the preview stands until your render arrives.
    </p>
    <p>
        The handles count in cells, and their <code>aria-valuemin</code> is a panel's least size. The camera
        carries <code>data-panel-ratio="16:9"</code>, so whichever handle moves it keeps that shape to the
        nearest row.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<h2 id="arranging-from-the-keyboard">Arranging from the keyboard <CatAnchor Id="arranging-from-the-keyboard" /></h2>
<div class="sedna-scroll-x">
    <table class="table">
        <thead>
            <tr><th>On</th><th>Key</th><th>Does</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td>a panel's grip</td><td><kbd class="kbd">Space</kbd> <kbd class="kbd">Enter</kbd></td><td>Picks the panel up; while it is held, drops it.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>a held panel</td><td><kbd class="kbd">←</kbd> <kbd class="kbd">→</kbd></td><td>A column towards the inline start or end, mirrored right to left.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>a held panel</td><td><kbd class="kbd">↑</kbd> <kbd class="kbd">↓</kbd></td><td>To the next row where it lands somewhere else: above or below the panel in the way.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>a held panel</td><td><kbd class="kbd">Esc</kbd>, or leaving it</td><td>Puts it back.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>a side or bottom handle</td><td>the arrows along it</td><td>A cell wider or narrower, taller or shorter; four with <kbd class="kbd">Shift</kbd>.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>
<p>
    Each step is read out through your <code>[data-drag-live]</code> region, as on
    <a href="/drag-drop#keyboard">drag and drop</a>; on a panel grid <code>{column}</code> and
    <code>{row}</code> name the cell, from 1. A handle is a separator whose <code>aria-valuenow</code> you
    render from the arrangement, so its new size is read when your render arrives.
    <code>sednaUi.panels.arrange(panels, columns, moved)</code> is the same layout in script, for a page
    without Blazor.
</p>

<h2 id="tall-panel">One tall panel <CatAnchor Id="tall-panel" /></h2>
<p>
    A grid row is as tall as its tallest panel. One long list beside three short tiles leaves holes
    beside the list and pushes the next row down by the difference. There are four ways out, from
    the smallest change to the biggest.
</p>
<ul>
    <li><code>.sedna-self-start</code> on the short panels keeps their own height. The row is still as tall.</li>
    <li><code>.sedna-row-span-2</code>, <code>.sedna-row-span-3</code> and <code>.sedna-row-span-4</code> let the tall panel take several rows, and the short ones fill the rows beside it.</li>
    <li><code>.sedna-stack</code> makes each column independent, as in the dashboard above.</li>
    <li><code>.sedna-grid-24--rows</code> gives every row the same height, so nothing can move at all.</li>
</ul>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Grid.RowSpan" Title="`.sedna-row-span-2`" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        The rota is two rows tall and the four queues fill the two rows beside it, two by two.
        <code>.card--fill</code> on every panel makes each end on its row's line.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Grid.RowSpanTall" Title="`.sedna-row-span-3` and `.sedna-row-span-4`" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        The same idea for a panel three or four rows tall: the short cards beside it take one row each.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Grid.FixedRows" Title="`.sedna-grid-24--rows`" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        A board of tiles: every row is <code>--grid-row</code> (9rem) tall whatever is in it, so a
        panel's height is its row span and nothing else. That is the layout monitoring dashboards
        use, and the only one in which a panel filling up moves nothing. What does not fit scrolls
        inside a <code>.card--fill</code>'s body while its head and foot stay in view — the queue here
        has more items than its two rows show. Put a list in a <code>.card-body--flush</code> so it
        is the body that scrolls.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Grid.Dense" Title="`.sedna-grid-24--dense`" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        Two 16-column panels leave an 8-column hole beside each. <code>--dense</code> fills it with
        the next panel that fits, so 3 moves up beside 1. <strong>The markup order is still the
        reading and tab order</strong>, so a dense grid reads out of visual order: use it for tiles
        that are peers, never for a sequence.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Grid.Grid24" Title="`.sedna-grid-24`" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        Twenty-four columns. <code>.sedna-span-1</code> to <code>.sedna-span-24</code> give a child
        that many of them, and a child with no span takes the whole row. The even steps —
        <code>.sedna-span-2</code>, <code>-4</code>, <code>-6</code> … <code>-24</code> — are a
        twelve-column grid, if twelfths are how you think; the odd ones are there for the layouts
        twelve cannot divide.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>It collapses on its own width, not the viewport's.</strong> Narrower than 48rem, a
        span under 8 becomes 12, so tiles pair up, and a span of 8 or more takes the row. Narrower
        than 30rem, everything takes the row. A <code>.sedna-grid-24</code> inside a span collapses
        by its own width. It is a size container, so it takes its width from where it sits: inside a
        flex row, give it <code>.sedna-fill</code>.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Grid.Split" Title="`.sedna-split`" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        A main column and an aside. The child marked <code>.sedna-split-aside</code> takes
        <code>--split-aside</code> (18rem; <code>.sedna-split--sm</code> is 14rem,
        <code>.sedna-split--lg</code> is 24rem), and the rest share what is left. The aside goes on
        whichever side it sits in the markup. When the main column would drop below
        <code>--split-main</code> (60%), the two stack.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Grid.SplitOptions" Title="Stacking at a set width, a sticky aside, two even columns" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.sedna-split--stack-sm</code>, <code>.sedna-split--stack-md</code> and <code>.sedna-split--stack-lg</code>
        stack the split below 560, 900 or 1200 pixels of its own width, and keep it side by side
        above, whatever the content. With one of them, <code>--sticky</code> keeps the aside in view
        while the main column scrolls, and <code>--aside-first</code> puts the aside first once the
        two stack. <code>.sedna-split--even</code> is two equal columns, each at least
        <code>--split-aside</code>.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Grid.ResizableSplit" Title="A split the reader resizes" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        A <code>.split-resize</code> handle between the two writes <code>--split-aside</code> as
        the reader drags it, with <code>data-resize-measure</code> naming the aside it measures. It
        sits in the gap and takes no room. Pair it with a <code>--stack-*</code> modifier, so the
        handle goes when the two stack.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Grid.AutoFill" Title="`.sedna-grid`" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        Peers in as many columns as <code>--grid-min</code> (280px) allows. It fills with empty tracks,
        so three children in a wide grid stay three tracks wide instead of stretching across the row.
        <code>.sedna-grid--sm</code> sets the minimum to 200px and <code>.sedna-grid--lg</code> to
        340px. <code>--grid-max</code> caps the number of columns however wide the grid is.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Grid.AutoFit" Title="`.sedna-grid--fit`" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        With <code>--fit</code> the empty tracks collapse, so the children that <em>are</em> there
        stretch to fill the row. With <code>.sedna-start</code> added, each child keeps its own height
        instead of stretching to the tallest in its row, which is what cards of very different
        lengths want.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Grid.FieldGrid" Title="Fields side by side" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.sedna-grid sedna-grid--sm sedna-grid--fit</code> is the form row: fields side by side,
        dropping to one column below about 200px each. Use it for values that belong together, like a
        from/to pair or a host and port.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Grid.SpanFull" Title="`.sedna-span-full`" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        One child taking the whole row of any grid: a chart over a row of tiles, the odd card that
        would otherwise sit alone at one track wide, or the <a href="/form">long field</a> among two-up
        fields. <strong>You decide which child that is.</strong> CSS cannot count the children against
        the tracks.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<div class="callout callout--warn">
    <i class="ri-arrow-left-right-line"></i>
    <div class="callout-body">
        <span class="callout-title">Renamed</span>
        <code>.card-grid</code> is now <code>.sedna-grid</code>. <code>.card-masonry</code> is now
        <code>.sedna-grid sedna-grid--fit sedna-grid--lg sedna-start</code>. <code>.field-grid</code> is
        now <code>.sedna-grid sedna-grid--sm sedna-grid--fit</code>. The old names are gone, not
        aliased.
    </div>
</div>
